# Test: a genuine RADIUS overflow refuses the whole daemon route update.
# QUESTION: does a 65-valid-route Access-Accept reach the actual admitted PPP
# subscriber's reactor, refuse redistribution atomically, preserve the useful
# 64-route other Birth and replay tokens, and retain original diagnostics on
# the daemon's registered error-history and doctor command surfaces?
# Why not a unit: direct observer calls cannot prove RADIUS extraction,
# metadata custody, real CHAP/IPCP, reactor publication and daemon RPC wiring.
# The existing unit roots own private counters, record pointers and the
# existing-Birth interleaving. This .ci makes no native counter/cleanup claim.
#
# Two real UDP LAC peers use the existing radius-acct-wire PPPoL2TP helper.
# Its CHAP/IPCP reaches the production Driver; no event or observer callback is
# used to manufacture successful authorization. The independent RADIUS mock
# returns 64 then65 valid routes, keyed by actual opaque NAS-Port-Id. Each real
# SessionIPAssigned must carry the exact original Birth, wire coordinates and
# full route count. An external observer subscribes before admission, records
# actual route-change events and requests the existing registered replay.
# Request quiesce joins plugin event application after the producer barrier;
# it is not used to guess that an unestablished peer exists. No BGP peer is
# part of this question. Native route source tokens are observed in the
# daemon's route events, not inferred from a successful delivery count.
#
# The first peer remains served while the second negotiates and diagnostics
# are read. Every new observer wait is bounded state Poll; all serving loops
# are canceled and joined before their owned sockets close. The observer uses
# the existing startup/failure-sentinel/shutdown flow. No skip-kernel-probe.
# A SKIP or setup-only run is not evidence for this test.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
    external route-overflow-test {
        run "le test fixture l2tp/radius-framed-route-overflow $PORT"
        encoder json
    }
}
l2tp {
    enabled true
    shared-secret s3cr3t
    auth-method chap-md5
    ncp {
        enable-ipv6cp false
    }
    auth {
        radius {
            nas-identifier overflow-lns
            nas-port-id-format "{tunnel-id}.{session-id}"
            timeout 5
            retries 2
            server mock {
                address 127.0.0.1
                port $PORT2
                accounting-port $PORT2
                shared-key testing123
            }
        }
    }
    pool {
        ipv4 {
            gateway 10.99.9.1
            start 10.99.9.10
            end 10.99.9.11
        }
    }
}
environment {
    l2tp {
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp.auth.radius:value=debug
option=env:var=TEST_SECRET:value=s3cr3t
option=timeout:value=180s

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture l2tp/radius-framed-route-overflow/radius $PORT2 testing123
# The observer is an external daemon plugin. Its assertion error reaches the
# runner through ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL before it requests the daemon's shutdown.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config:timeout=180s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:pattern=level=WARN.*code=doctor-l2tp-framed-route-limit
expect=stderr:contains=OK: real RADIUS 65-route refusal preserved the useful 64-route Birth and exact replay tokens; daemon errors and doctor retained original context
